/target
# Build output of the standalone BDK canary crate (scripts/canary/bdk-canary).
# Its Cargo.lock IS committed (reproducible downstream pin); its target/ is not.
/scripts/canary/bdk-canary/target

# Same for the reference push relay (contrib/push-relay): a standalone crate
# excluded from the workspace, so the root /target rule does not cover it. The
# rule lives this early in the stack, ahead of the crate it protects, because
# 831 MiB of its build output was once committed by a stray `git add -A` four
# PRs before the crate itself existed — and .gitignore does not untrack what is
# already tracked.
/contrib/push-relay/target
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/
.cargo/config.toml

# Nix build outputs (`nix build` symlinks; flake repl history)
result
result-*
.direnv/

# CycloneDX SBOMs from local `cargo cyclonedx` runs. CI generates and
# uploads these as release artifacts; never check them in.
*.cdx.json
*.cdx.json.sha256

# Bitcoin Core functional-test harness (contrib/core-functional). The Core
# tree is fetched from the pinned tag by fetch-core.sh, never vendored; the
# config.ini handed to Core's framework is generated per run by run.sh.
# The trailing-slash form matches a directory only, so a symlink named `core`
# — which is how fetch-core.sh can leave it when Core lives elsewhere — was
# not ignored and got committed once. Match both shapes.
/contrib/core-functional/core
/contrib/core-functional/core/
# Core's framework seeds a cached chain next to the tests; run.sh redirects it
# out of the tree, but a hand-run test would otherwise drop it here.
/contrib/core-functional/cache/
